policy-boundary: |
  THE ENGINE IS THE KIT'S. THE POLICY IS THE REPO'S.

  Seeded (kit-owned, refreshed by --upgrade, improved upstream):
    scripts/issues/*.py — classification math, batching, the OODA budget gate,
    the verify-and-close gate, and the tests.

  NEVER written by the kit (repo-owned, hand-edited):
    .issues/config.yml   — dispositions, label namespace, limits,
                           resolve_allow_globs, `features:` flags
    .issues/budget.yml   — backpressure caps
    .issues/README.md, .issues/reports/, .issues/worklists/

  fanout.sh has no code path that writes any file under .issues/. That is the
  boundary: a repo can adopt a newer engine without any risk to its policy, and
  a policy change never needs an engine change. A bug in the engine is fixed
  once, upstream; a misrouted issue is fixed in one repo's config.yml.

  Corollary: DO NOT hand-edit a seeded scripts/issues/*.py. --upgrade refreshes
  a file only when it is byte-identical to the current kit shape or an archived
  one, so a local edit permanently strands that copy — which is exactly how the
  two forks this kit replaces came to exist.
changelog: |
  0.1.0 — initial kit (FF-0018). Extracts the issue-triage engine that
  bamr87/it-journey and bamr87/zer0-mistakes each maintained a fork of, into one
  canonical engine seeded as an OPT-IN artifact (`--artifacts issue-autopilot`,
  never in the default set).

  MEASURED DIVERGENCE at extraction time (full diff, not an estimate):
    dispatch.py — BYTE-IDENTICAL across both forks (17883 bytes each).
    triage.py   — 78 added / 8 changed lines, all on the zer0-mistakes side,
                  in three features:
                    (a) the verify-and-close lane (`verify_candidate` flag,
                        its count, the status line, the docstring),
                    (b) `any_of` OR-groups in disposition matching,
                    (c) the `skip` action -> a "Left alone (protected)" batch
                        and its worklist section.
    zer0-mistakes only: verify_close.py, test_verify_close.py.
    it-journey only: nothing.

  CORRECTION to the founding issue: batching is present in BOTH forks, textually
  identical apart from (c). It was never a fork-exclusive feature, so nothing
  about it needed reconciling and no flag was added for it.

  CONVERGENCE RULE — a feature is flagged only when it CHANGES BEHAVIOUR for a
  repo that has not asked for it:
    (a) verify-and-close is FLAGGED (`features.verify_close`, default OFF). It
        is the only path by which the autopilot ever closes a HUMAN-authored
        issue; no repo may acquire it by adopting a kit. With the flag off the
        engine emits no `verify_candidate` key and no `verify_candidates` count,
        so plan.json is byte-identical to a pre-kit run rather than merely
        equivalent.
    (b) and (c) are UNCONDITIONAL. Both are inert unless a repo's config.yml
        actually uses them (`any_of` in a match; `action: skip` on a
        disposition), so flagging them would add a knob with no off-state
        behaviour to protect. Proven by test_triage_engine.py.

  ONE DELIBERATE GENERALIZATION: verify candidacy excluded
  `disposition_id != "backlog-managed"` — one repo's disposition id baked into a
  shared engine. The kit excludes `action != "skip"` instead, which selects
  exactly the same issues under zer0-mistakes' config (its only `skip`
  disposition IS `backlog-managed`) and keeps a renamed or additional protected
  disposition protected. Covered by a test.

  PARITY IS TESTED, NOT ASSERTED. Both pre-kit engines are archived verbatim,
  and test_triage_engine.py runs them beside the canonical engine over the same
  fixtures with each repo's real disposition rules, asserting plan.json AND the
  rendered worklist Markdown are equal — lane off == it-journey, lane on ==
  zer0-mistakes.
notes: |
  ADOPTION, per repo, is one fan-out plus at most one hand-edited line:
    it-journey     --artifacts issue-autopilot --upgrade   (no config change:
                   the lane defaults off, which is what it does today)
    zer0-mistakes  --artifacts issue-autopilot --upgrade   plus `features:
                   {verify_close: true}` in .issues/config.yml — the kit will
                   not write it, by design.
  Both forks' current engine bytes are in archive/, so --upgrade recognizes them
  as machine seeds and converts them in place.

  The four SKELETONS are additive-only and both repos already have hand-authored
  skill/agent files, so a fan-out will NOT overwrite them. They exist for the
  next repo to adopt the loop; they carry TODO(adopt) markers where per-repo
  policy has to be filled in.
